INSERT OR IGNORE INTO articles (id,title,slug,category,excerpt,content,author,authorTitle,tags,readTime,published,publishedAt,updatedAt) VALUES
('10','Sections 122A and 122B of the Transfer of Property Act 1882: Bangladesh''s 2026 Amendment on Lifetime Usufruct and Heba','sections-122a-122b-tpa-1882-bangladesh-2026-lifetime-usufruct-heba','property-law','Bangladesh''s 2026 amendment to the Transfer of Property Act 1882 inserted Sections 122A and 122B, creating a statutory framework for gifts with lifetime usufruct retention. This article examines the corpus-usufruct split mechanism, the irrevocability regime under Section 122B, the classical Heba doctrine and the ayn-manafi distinction, and the savings clause that preserves Muslim personal law alongside the new statutory route.','Across Bangladesh, a quiet crisis has been unfolding for decades. Elderly parents, trusting in filial duty, gift their homes and lands to their children only to find themselves dispossessed, evicted, or reduced to dependents in the very houses they built. The law offered no structured mechanism for a parent to transfer property while retaining the right to live in it, collect its rents, or enjoy its income for life.

In 2026, Bangladesh''s Jatiya Sangsad enacted an amendment to the Transfer of Property Act 1882 (TPA) inserting **Sections 122A and 122B of the Transfer of Property Act 1882**, creating a statutory framework for gifts with lifetime usufruct retention. This is a Bangladeshi enactment. Both Bangladesh and India inherited the TPA 1882 from British colonial legislation, but the two countries have diverged substantially in their amendments. India has no equivalent provision.

Section 122A allows a donor to transfer the corpus of property to a donee while retaining enumerated usufruct rights for life. Section 122B governs irrevocability and the narrow grounds for revocation. Section 122A(4) contains a savings clause that expressly preserves Heba under Muslim personal law.

## Legislative Context: Why Bangladesh Amended the Transfer of Property Act in 2026

The social mischief that prompted this amendment is neither obscure nor debatable. Elderly donors who transferred immovable property to their children had no legally enforceable right to continue occupying or deriving income from that property. The existing TPA framework treated a completed gift as an absolute transfer.

Bangladesh''s legislative trajectory with the TPA has been independent since 1971. The 2026 amendment is the most significant reform to the gift provisions. India addressed elderly vulnerability through the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007, which empowers tribunals to cancel transfers but creates no usufruct retention mechanism.

## Section 122A Explained: Gift with Lifetime Usufruct Retention

Section 122A limits its application to specified relationships: parent and child, grandparent and grandchild, and spouses. The mechanism operates through a corpus-usufruct split. The corpus transfers to the donee immediately upon registration. The donor retains enumerated usufruct rights: occupation, rents, and income. The usufruct is a real right, not merely a personal licence — it attaches to the property and binds successors in title.

## Section 122B Explained: Irrevocability, Revocation Grounds, and Judicial Oversight

The default rule under Section 122B is irrevocability. A registered gift under Section 122A cannot be revoked unilaterally by the donor. Revocation is permitted only by mutual consent documented in a registered deed specifying genuine necessity, or by judicial intervention where the donee''s consent genuinely cannot be obtained.

## Heba Under Muslim Personal Law: Classical Doctrine and the Ayn-Manafi Distinction

In both India and Bangladesh, Heba is governed by Muslim personal law, not the TPA. The three essentials of a valid Heba are Ijab (declaration), Qabul (acceptance), and Qabza (delivery of possession). No registration or writing is required.

The ayn-manafi distinction is the critical doctrinal key. Muslim jurisprudence strictly distinguishes between the corpus (ayn) and its usufruct (manafi). A Hiba that transfers the corpus unconditionally while the donor retains the usufruct for life is valid under Muslim law.

## Does Section 122A Affect Heba?

The apparent conflict is largely illusory. Section 122A(4) expressly states the section does not restrict or affect ordinary gifts, hiba under Muslim law or any other legally recognised method of property transfer. Section 122A is elective, not mandatory.

## Frequently Asked Questions on Sections 122A and 122B TPA 1882

### Does Section 122A of the TPA apply in India?

No. Section 122A was enacted by Bangladesh''s Jatiya Sangsad in 2026. India has no equivalent provision.

### Can a Muslim donor in Bangladesh use Section 122A instead of Heba?

Yes. Section 122A(4) preserves Heba. A Muslim donor may choose either route.

### Is a gift under Section 122A revocable?

Irrevocable by default under Section 122B. Revocation is permitted only by mutual consent documented in a registered deed specifying genuine necessity, or by judicial intervention where the donee''s consent genuinely cannot be obtained.

### What happens if the donee dies before the donor?

The donee''s heirs inherit the corpus. The donor''s usufruct rights survive and continue until the donor''s death.

### Does Section 122A override Islamic inheritance (faraiz) rules?

No. Section 122A governs inter vivos gifts, not testamentary succession.

### What is the difference between Section 122A and Section 126 TPA?

Section 126 governs general revocability and permits revocation where the gift deed contains an express revocation condition. Section 122B is stricter: it does not permit the donor to reserve a unilateral right of revocation.','Lamia Akhter','LLB Student & Legal Researcher','["property law","Bangladesh","TPA 1882","heba","usufruct","Muslim personal law","gift deed"]',13,1,'2026-10-05T09:00:00Z','2026-10-05T09:00:00Z');
